                    "Each occurrence of this entity represents a single organisation. An organisation is said to come into existence when it is incorporated or when another entity chooses to recognise it for some purpose. It will no longer exist if it is dissolved or if no other entities choose to recognise it. An organisation usually has a type that defines its function or purpose. There need only be one occurrence of this entity for each organisation no matter how many other entities reference it over time. It is not uncommon for there to be formal relationships between organisations. A common example is a global organisation which is comprised of multiple subsidiaries. We may refer to the regional organisations as sections or divisions. However, for the sake of this module, we define each entity independently. Other modules will address the structural relationship between different organisations."
